The preview-1 interface definition, and the script that derives wasm signatures from it.

THIS IS THE WITNESS'S DOMAIN, and it is derived from the specification rather than written by hand — the
lesson recorded when #264 closed: *derive the domain from the space, never from the registry.* A hand-kept
list of "the functions we supply" can only ever agree with itself.

  wasi_snapshot_preview1.witx
    source: https://github.com/WebAssembly/WASI
    branch: snapshot-01   (`main` no longer carries witx; the layout moved to specifications/)
    commit: 7256722d5d194be36842ba65d9983dff3718d87d
    path:   phases/snapshot/witx/wasi_snapshot_preview1.witx
    bytes:  19235
    sha256: f6e83cb553b40a34f6622343fbfbf5d24969383fad6a420fd45a29af762161e2

  derive-signatures.py
    Maps each `(@interface func (export "name"))` to a wasm signature under the canonical ABI: params by
    type, the FIRST $errno result becomes the wasm return, and ADDITIONAL results become trailing i32
    out-pointers.

    Run: python3 derive-signatures.py wasi_snapshot_preview1.witx out.json

VALIDATED AGAINST KNOWN-GOOD, which is the only reason it is trusted for the names the host did NOT already
have. All 27 signatures the host already declared in a parseable form must agree, and they do — **27 agree,
0 disagree**. Two earlier versions of the script FAILED that check and were caught by it rather than by
reading the output:

  1. it skipped `(param $path string)` — a builtin witx type carries no `$`, so every string param
     vanished and `path_link` derived 3 params where the truth is 7;
  2. it skipped `(param $buf (@witx pointer u8))` — a nested type form — so `random_get`, `fd_readdir`,
     `poll_oneoff`, `args_get` and `environ_get` each lost a param.

Neither failure was visible in the output on its own. Both were visible the moment the derived set was
compared with signatures already known to work.

ONE DOCUMENTED ADDITION BEYOND THIS SPEC: `sock_accept` is **not** in snapshot-01's witx, and Go's wasip1
port imports it (`src/syscall/net_wasip1.go`, `//go:wasmimport wasi_snapshot_preview1 sock_accept`). It is a
post-snapshot addition, so the witness's domain is *this witx plus that name*, with the addition named rather
than folded in — a guest may import it, so the host must supply it.
